{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["The reason for <b>there they buried Isaac</b> — that he did not say 'I buried' — is because Esau was with him at the burial of their father, and he does not now wish to mention him. Moreover, he did not need now to elaborate and say, 'there we buried Isaac and there they buried Rebekah his wife,' for he was not present at his mother's burial. And in his charge he mentioned the cave and those buried in it, to exalt the place, so that they would hasten to bury him there. And he said, 'which Abraham acquired with the field for a burial possession,' to inform them that Abraham commanded that that place be a burial house for them as an everlasting possession. But below (Genesis 50:13), when it says, 'And they buried him in the cave of the field of Machpelah which Abraham acquired for a burial possession,' Scripture intended to hint that with Jacob the intention of the righteous was fulfilled, for it was acquired for the three of them and no one else would be buried in it anymore. And therefore Joseph did not command that he be buried in the cave with his fathers. I saw in the Mechilta of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai (Beshalach 19), that Joseph said to them: when you bring me up, bury me in any place you wish — I accept that I shall not enter the burial of my fathers, for none enter the burial of the fathers except three patriarchs and three matriarchs, as it is said, 'there they buried Abraham' etc., and it says (Genesis 50:5), 'in my grave which I dug for myself' — with me it terminates. And it is possible that he hinted to them by saying 'and there I buried Leah,' that he already took possession of the cave, and he said this against Esau, lest he and his sons contest his burial, claiming that the cave belongs to him, for he is the firstborn and he is the one fit to be buried with his fathers — and even though he went to another land, they would carry him from there just as his sons would carry Jacob, for he would wish to be buried with his holy fathers and to be joined with them in burial; and if Esau is buried there, Jacob would not be buried there, for two families do not share one burial house. And this too is what he meant by 'in my grave which I dug for myself' — he had already dug a grave for himself to take possession of it. And this is the meaning of 'and there went up with him both chariots and horsemen' (Genesis 50:9), for he knew the insolence of Esau and his sons. And so the matter came to pass, for we find in the book of the Chronicles of Yosef ben Gurion (which is the book of Josippon) and other books of antiquity, that Zepho son of Eliphaz son of Esau came and clashed with them over this until they waged war, and the hand of Joseph prevailed, and they captured him with the choicest of his legions and brought them to Egypt, and he remained there in prison all the days of Joseph, and he escaped from there upon Joseph's death, and went to the land of Chaffania and ruled over Kittim in Rome, and in the end he was made king over all the land of Italy, and he was the first to rule over Rome, and he was the one who built the first and greatest temple built in Rome. And our Sages as well (Sotah 13a) mentioned this matter of the clash of Esau at the cave. And the verse stated here (Genesis 50:14), 'And Joseph returned to Egypt, he and his brothers and all who went up with him to bury his father, after he had buried his father,' comes to hint that not a single one of them died in the war or on the road, for the merit of the patriarch and the merit of Joseph who went up with him stood for them. And Joseph did not command, as his father commanded, to carry him for burial in the land at once, for the house of Pharaoh would not permit it, for it is an honor to them. Moreover, if his brothers and his father's house were to go with him, the people of the land would plunder and steal everything they have. And it is not the way of honor that others should transport him there. n: 'קטטה' rendered as 'clashed/clash'; the word can mean quarrel or physical altercation; the context involves armed conflict so 'clash' captures both senses." "And Joseph said to his brothers, 'Behold, I am dying' (Genesis 50:24), because his brothers were still alive, for they lived longer than he did, as you see in the case of Levi. And it says (Genesis 50:25), 'And Joseph swore the children of Israel,' because when he saw that his brothers were old, he adjured all their children and all his father's house that they would also command their children's children at the time of the redemption, for they all know of the exile."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:47:33.120742382Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}